first order is torn off, the second is dated June 18, 1690 : — . • • • • <«-the first line, the generall to be beate att 3 Clock in y* morning, att 4 y* asemble to be mustrd by y* inspectors, y* second line nott to shut, y* gards as usuall, y* pickets as usuall; none of y* foote to put there horses in aney medow, and must not send there horses beyond y* horse gards, y* retrete to beate att 9 stt night, & take it from y* gards. i ^ y* regments named last night for y* gards of y* generall officers must furnish them exactly. Each Lef^ generalls garde must be 30 men, Lef^ &c My Lord Tirconell and Ms^er Delosone* must have 40 men eadi, a CapS a ^ De Lauzun. 169 Lef S 2 sargens, & a Drum, ICageir Deloflone requireB none tonight No soldier to ease himself between y* lines. 13 men w* a sargent to goeto y* to parade in y* same place y« same time: Westmeath, Tirone, y* Grand Prior's, & Grordian O Neale^ to famish each 30 men, y* Grand Priors' a LeT Coll., each of the other three a CapS LeP, 2 Sargents and a Drum to be att y* hed of y* gards to releive a Lef * GolL, and a like number of men att Mara Castell* on y* rode to y* Nnrey .... and 5 men to be brought immedeatelyto y* Coll. of y* Grand Priors Regement, w' a sargent, & a Drum from Westmeaths rega- ment, the gards to mount at y* broken brige att y* head of y* first line, and they are to draw up when y* horse gard marches to there gard post S* Peeter is y* word. pickett 40 men. 8 men a Cap* & an Insign att 3 o Clock next morning to meet att y* gards at y* hed of y* gard June y* 18**^, Orders, 1690 The generall to beate att 4, the assemble when ordered, the gards for Moyra to be relieved by 30 men from O Bryan,^ Bagnell,' Hamilton' and Bellu ;^ O bryan Ler ColL ; a Capt* and subalteme from each, w* Drum, 2 sargens, att 3 o Clock, to be att j* head of y* gards to relieve y* like num- ber at Moyra Castle on y* rode to y* Nurey, y* detachment for y* horses as usually is att 3 o Clock in y* morning: when y* gards beate y* as- sembly. . . . are to of att y* Magiors to be att y* head of y* gards to reaceave orders in y* morning. Simpitar Brigadere for y* day, Lord Bellu Coll. ; Hamilton Lef CoU. LeT Generall Hamilton lost a guide wach w^ sesles to it, if anney souldier y* found it shall have lO' for his pains, and if enney officer bought it he shall be returned his money.« y* word S* Poule. Order June y* 19. 1690 The incame [?] regular is to be 300 foote distans, bey streete 2 foote, the large streete 15, the fire of souldiers to be 12, y* Ler 20 from y* fire, y* Cap* 45 feete b^inde y* Lef^, the ColL to be 15 pases behind y* Cap- tins, y' LeT Coll. and Mager to be in a line 2 pases before y* Coll. The campe garde to l^ 100 pases from y* stand of armea 20 pases from y* fronte tent, to y* reare tent Orders y* 20 of June 1690 100 men to be sent on y* left of yj armey w* 2 Cap^ 2 Lef ^ 2 Ensigns, and 4 Sargens: 150 firelocks, 2 Cap^, 2 Lef **, 2 Ensigns, 6 Sargens w* a dayes brewi for them all; 50 men toDrumgule Castell, w* a Cap*, Ler, & Ensigne, & 2 Sargens who is to bring pen, inke, and paper w* him, to write to y* LeT Generall of y* day what hapens; 120 men to Moyra > Gordon O'Neill's regiment of foot. » Dudley BagnalFs regjiment of foot * Grand Frior*s regiment of foot > John Hamilton's regiment of foot. ' Moyra Castle. ^ Lord Bellew's regiment of foot. * Charles O'Bryan's regiment of foot. ^ This is a emious entry. 170 Castle, a heP ColL, 3 Captins, 3 subalterns, 6 Sargens, and 3 Drums; all these to parade at y* bed of Antrimes Regment att 3 o Clock in y* morning. Grand Prior, Louth, and Gordian O Neale, Antrim & Bellu to furnish y* above number. Wacobb Brigadere for y* day. Bercon [?] ColL for y* day. When the King goes bye y* line, y* picte garde is to draw out, y* of- ficers to salute. The Word is S* Andrew. A few days before the battle of the Boyne, James encamped at Cookestown, near Ardee. Here we have the list of regiments and the order of the encampment. This entry dates a short time after the General Orders just given. One of the leaves in which the entry is made is imperfect at the bottom, which may account for (jafhey's regiment not being mentioned : — Camp of Ardee y* 24'^ of June 1690. The first line on y* right Seaven troops of Cards \ 2 Duke of Tirconnell Regment of hors / Three Batalions of y* H^yall Regment .... 51 companeys, compute three Regments . . . Lord Antrim Lord Bellu Grordian O Neale Lord of Louth Granprior Seaven of ffrench each com* 16 companeys ^ Regm* . . 7 Grolmay*8^ Regment of hors com* nine traps .... 1 Maxfild's' Regment of Draguns 1 In Ardee ColL Case* and y* to Coll. M'' Mahons* ... 3 Second line on y* right Lord of Clare his Regment of Draguns 1 Sunderland his R^ment 5 traps I Parkars Regment of Hors 1 Hamiltons foote Lord of Westmeath Sir Michael Cregh^ Mahgilicutt^ O Bryan Buslo Bagnall Lord of Tirone ' Lord Galmoy. * Art and Hugh. * Maxwell's. * Creagh. ' Colonel Richard Grace. ^ Denis Mac G^cnddy. 8 171 Mangan* his R^gement of DragunB 5 Regments of hors 5 .... Draguns 3 W"* ColL Sarsfild Coll. Sarsfild Begxnent of hors Aprukoras* hors Clifords Draguns Sir Neale O Neales Dragons Coll. Carrolls Draguns Foote Lord of Slane Dilon Clanrickard Gralwaj Borke Nugent Cormonstowne' The MS. contains seyeral other entries, bu|; not of suffident inter- est to be transferred to the immortality of print; I camiot, however, resist giving one more extract, especially as it throws some light on the sanitary condition of James' forces, showing the prevalence of that dedmator of armies in former times in Ir^and — ague ; in the fly-leaf of Captain Ga&ey's pocket-book we find the following by no means disagreeable — Cure for y* Eague. One oimce of cortex newe, one Dram of powder of snake weed, one dram of powder of nutmegs made up into an electuary, with a sufficient quan- tity of syrup of lemmons, you are to take the bigness of a chesnut of it three or four times in y* four and twenty hours, whilst it [the ague] lasts shaking, after it a glass of claret, or milk warmed mixed with bran<]y and sugar. Whilst placing the foregoing extracts in what seemed to be their natural sequence, I have purposely refi»ined firom entering on the general history of the stirring times to which thev refer, dthouffh many a temptmg open for historical disquisition is afibrded by we various inciaental notices which occur m tiie <^ jottings" ofCajp- tain Ga&e^. In Harris' Life of William III., and tiie graphic and impartial narrative of Storey, the general history of the period has had ample justice done it, whUst tiie details have been illustrated by the publication, in our own day, of the *^ Macariss Exddium, 1 Lord DuDgan? ' Aberoorn. > Gormanslowii. »t 172 edited for the Irish ArchaBological Sodety, with copioos and valuable notes and appendices, by Mr. O'Callagban. But although the pro- minent features of the picture may have been well and clearly painted, yet it must be confessed that the minor details are sadly defective. Mr. John D' Alton has lately announced the publication of an original Army List of James the Second's forces (should sufficient encou- ragement be afforded by the public), to be illustrated by an histori- cal and genealogical commentanr ; Mr. O'Calk^han also promises a history of the services of the Lish Brigade abroad. It is to be hoped, that when the labours of these gentlemen see the light many desiderata will be supplied : but even though the publication of the works may be all that the character of their authors would lead us to expect, I cannot refrain from expressing a wish that the historian of the period could refer to many documents of a nature similar to CMEctejs memoranda, that so he might be the better enabled to warm into life and action the vague shadows of the past I RrFCRCNaS TOTNC DETAILS m PLAN. JfibfTno-lTaBDaii. Shadni thu. ■ IIU. I ^DtUTf. or £arl^ Erig|iii, MmA. Decorated Jha^an a I, Modem, inOalimt. t i "Dcstroj^d Portions, Jfetied. A DKtz^feJl'MTower,£ber&o-NaDiua